{
  "image_name": "1c58638e7c8f586afc157cc378f72512.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how gut dysbiosis can trigger local and systemic inflammation. At the top, an altered microbial community in the mucus layer sits over the intestinal epithelium. This imbalance appears to weaken the epithelial barrier, including tight-junction disruption mediated by zonulin, leading to epithelial cell death and gut inflammation. As the barrier breaks down, bacterial products such as LPS pass into the submucosa and even the bloodstream.\n\nThese microbial signals activate inflammatory pathways in intestinal immune cells. A central event shown is pyroptosis, which releases IL-1β and IL-18. These cytokines then stimulate several innate and innate-like immune populations, including macrophages, follicular dendritic cells, ILC3, MAIT cells, and γδ T cells. Macrophages and dendritic cells are linked to IL-23 production, while downstream responses include IL-17 and IL-22, suggesting amplification of mucosal inflammation. The figure also indicates formation of isolated lymphoid follicles within an inflamed lamina propria.\n\nAt the bottom, inflammatory mediators and bacterial components entering blood vessels are associated with broader inflammatory cell death and dissemination beyond the gut, with arrows pointing toward lymph nodes, entheses, and joints. Overall, the image presents a barrier-breach model connecting dysbiosis to immune activation and extraintestinal inflammation.",
